#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "c427d9b4-2944-5edc-8e19-363de348d23d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15147" :question/problem "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n2. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/0fc4f6ac431f4a118406f367a2049f25.png)" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin$:\n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/ceedec3a772973eda45dc8e75ea8a1b4.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/7d3a45be0fad34026a756e0685ef72ed.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C$. Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/f3f7b1aa12b9af600ca4107f8ba7963e.png)\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0$.\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/f7f6796435787eeb8416a755b211bebc.png)\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n$\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for Flux"}] :question/id #uuid "15426c37-1508-5407-ab49-b26f8761795c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15148" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4$. If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j$.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/abb3c6599e722f86db8e3d48613846f1.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)(8yi + 4xj) \\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i + \\frac{y}{2}j)ds \\\\\n&= ∮_(C)4xy + 2xyds \\\\\n&= ∮_(C)6xyds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a82102-6dd6-5a73-8370-9050e0f2323a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15149" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O$, as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/4ba14e820f544a5113eaea401b1ac37f.png)\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/16258660947a1a027a68b4d411919586.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1$. We have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds &= ∫_{C_{1}}(2xi + 2yj) \\cdot ids \\\\\n&= 2∫_{C_{1}}xds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∫_{C_{1}}xds &= 2∫_{-1}^{1}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt \\\\\n&= 2∫_{-1}^{1}dt \\\\\n&= 2(1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3$, and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Rectangle Using Two Symmetry Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "3f592794-e0e9-529c-9040-7b2806de2380" :question/math-academy-id "e-15150" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/14fbee42939abe16d9fd3f09b62b7242.png)\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/8550241bbdc42d8ce5e3d1b6f1658d69.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1$. We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i$, and therefore,\n$∫_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds|= ∫_{C_{1}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot ids; = 5∫_{C_{1}}xds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]$.\nTherefore,\n$5∫_{C_{1}}xds|= 5∫_{-1}^{1}2 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; = 10∫_{-1}^{1}dt; = 10 \\cdot 2; = 20$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20$.\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2$. We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j$, and therefore,\n$∫_{C_{2}}F \\cdot n_{2}ds|= ∫_{C_{2}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot jds; =-3∫_{C_{2}}yds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2]$.\nTherefore,\n$-3∫_{C_{2}}yds|=-3∫_{-2}^{2}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; =-3∫_{-2}^{2}dt; = (-3) \\cdot 4; =-12$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12$.\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d22a2a4-68db-59b6-ad13-7eafa3667f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177779} {:answer/id #uuid "d48f61eb-cd90-5717-9c17-8f7d5b7056f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177780} {:answer/id #uuid "e909804d-49a6-5d88-9fb8-f8f4164349b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177781} {:answer/id #uuid "205ef4ed-8eed-5b45-afea-3777203b1457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177782} {:answer/id #uuid "47e21bd1-8ca1-59d8-9cea-f0496f7db8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d22a2a4-68db-59b6-ad13-7eafa3667f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177779} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c2c5a44-e192-55ce-b6b8-808eb20e4685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177778}] :question/id #uuid "505ec5b2-1b42-5150-bddf-6e7219956cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126728" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126728.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n2. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for Flux"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d612aa2-925d-5aa8-8d2b-3bc38ae95a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3} + y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177791} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a995c-e2a1-5747-9df8-39947a431054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177792} {:answer/id #uuid "d5417966-554e-5680-b4e7-dcb66d5b7836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3} - y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177793} {:answer/id #uuid "e37a180c-be8c-5853-be42-f4dfc22e1449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177794} {:answer/id #uuid "2378ede0-9fe7-533c-81cd-b4fbc19a932a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5417966-554e-5680-b4e7-dcb66d5b7836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3} - y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177793} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c2d21d3-40aa-5247-99db-ceceeae9976e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177790}] :question/id #uuid "4ba1404b-6716-5e6e-975f-e1670e68a51c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126833" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i - y^{2}j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$. If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$∮_{C}AAAAAAAds$,\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n = xi + yj$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f132a091-0c62-53f2-8c66-d1eedbd44192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177803} {:answer/id #uuid "fae94702-fd0b-58df-a008-422c51cd2ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177804} {:answer/id #uuid "03d28847-5e71-55d3-9c8d-421a17cac5cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177805} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcac3a1-5fde-5ed3-bbaf-9ae15ac7d30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177806} {:answer/id #uuid "37f05e31-4e34-5b39-8b4d-739768816522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03d28847-5e71-55d3-9c8d-421a17cac5cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177805} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2d13dd5-e5b0-5ade-b6c8-fc512ab98e4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177802}] :question/id #uuid "ea876586-e09c-5050-847d-bc8b312ea302" :question/math-academy-id "q-126881" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126881.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = 2j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$."}]}
